window.docContents[73].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.Semantics/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___Semantics":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA › Semantics.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the computational proof and symbolic proof 6 headline theorems depend on it 85 of 285 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nDeclarations on this page (8)def evalSvcstructure Programdef evalInstdef hookAtOff?def externAt?def fetchMeminductive Stepinductive Steps\n\nARM64 Small-Step Operational Semantics (Arm64.Semantics)* Layer / Role: Arm64 Trusted Computing Base (Role::Spec, definitions only, 0 theorems).* Purpose & Key Exports: Read-only relocated code image (Program), instruction fetch and decode from executable memory (Program.fetchMem) and from the static image (Program.fetch), code-in-memory invariant (Program.CodeLoaded), single-step transition relation (Step), and multi-step closure (Steps). The executable fuel-bounded evaluators (ProofLib.stepState, execN, execUntilPC) and the test-memory builder Program.loadCode are untrusted and live in ProofLib/Exec.lean.* When to read: Read when auditing how Step fetches from s.mem (fetchMem), dispatches externs/overrides hooks (Step.extern), or enforces Memory.KeepsFrozen. For proof stepping lemmas, see ProofLib/Semantics.lean.Instructions are fetched from memory (Program.fetchMem) at an aligned, executable pc. Because executable memory is never writable (canWrite requires ¬x) and every Step preserves frozen bytes (Memory.KeepsFrozen), Program.CodeLoaded is invariant across execution and Program.fetch agrees with Program.fetchMem on loaded states.\n\n\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 43–44): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.execInst\n\ndef evalSvcL46–53 ↗#Apply supervisor-call handler svc while preserving frozen memory (Perm.frozen), tpidrEl0, and symtab, matching architectural user-space supervisor-call invariants.\n\nstructure ProgramL55–71 ↗#A read-only AArch64 code image:* base: load address of code,* code: instruction bytes, with relocations applied,* symbols: defined symbols (demangled names) and their offsets from base,* stubs: extern functions and the offsets of their stubs,* externs: behaviour of extern functions, consulted at stub offsets,* overrides: replacement behaviour for defined functions, consulted at symbol offsets,* svc: supervisor-call (SVC #0) handler bundle, defaulting to Syscall.standard. See Step.extern and Step.exec.\n\n\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 75–86): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.ofSvc, Arm64.Program.withSvc\n\ndef evalInstL88–90 ↗#Execute a single decoded Instr under prog's supervisor-call handler (evalSvc prog.svc).\n\n6 declarations not shown (lines 92–122): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.wordToBytesLE, Arm64.Program.ofWords, Arm64.Program.findSymbol?, Arm64.Program.fnOff, Arm64.Program.fnAddr, Arm64.Program.rebase\n\ndef hookAtOff?L124–127 ↗#The hook at offset off: an extern at a stub, or an override at a defined symbol.\n\ndef externAt?L129–131 ↗#The hook, if any, whose entry point is pc.\n\n2 declarations not shown (lines 133–149): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.readWordOff, Arm64.Program.fetch\n\ndef fetchMemL151–159 ↗#Fetch and decode the instruction at pc from memory m: the 32-bit little-endian word at pc, which must be 4-byte aligned and executable (Memory.canExec); otherwise none (the real machine faults). At a hooked entry point fetchMem returns none, as fetch does.\n\n6 declarations not shown (lines 161–228): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.Program.CodeLoaded, Arm64.Program.hookFree, Arm64.Program.hasWordsOff, Arm64.Program.hasWords, Arm64.Program.hasCode, Arm64.Program.decidableForallLT\n\n\n\ninductive StepL232–268 ↗#Trusted small-step operational semantics relation Step prog s s'.At an extern entry point (prog.externAt? s.pc = some f) exactly one of extern / extern_fault applies (fetchMem returns none there); elsewhere exactly one of exec / halt_unmapped applies, so Step is deterministic.\n\ninductive StepsL270–273 ↗#Reflexive-transitive closure of Step.\n\n1 declaration not shown (lines 275–283): theorems, which Lean checks, or definitions that no headline statement usesArm64.StepsUntil, Arm64.StepsUntil.step, Arm64.StepsUntil.refl\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/ISA/Semantics.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.ISA.Semantics/#file-Arm64___Trusted___ISA___Semantics"}});